  "yt_description": "An UPDATE in PostgreSQL does not remove the old row right away.\n\n• Postgres keeps old row versions: multiversion concurrency control (MVCC)\n• Each statement sees a snapshot, so reading never blocks writing and writing never blocks reading\n• Old versions become dead rows that take disk space\n• VACUUM removes dead rows and marks the space for reuse\n• Plain VACUUM usually does not return space to the OS; VACUUM FULL does, with an exclusive lock\n\n#postgresql #databases #systemdesign #backend #softwareengineering",
  "ig_caption": "UPDATE in Postgres doesn't overwrite your row. 🤯\n\n🧬 It keeps the old version next to the new one (MVCC)\n👀 Readers never block writers, writers never block readers\n💀 Old versions pile up as dead rows\n🧹 VACUUM clears them, autovacuum runs it for you\n🔒 VACUUM FULL shrinks the table but locks it\n\nFollow @stackshade for more.\n\nVoiceover is AI-generated. Source: PostgreSQL documentation.",
